<div class="ltx_dates">© cc</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalized AI agents are becoming central to modern information retrieval, yet most evaluation methodologies remain static — relying on fixed benchmarks and one-off metrics that fail to reflect how users’ needs evolve over time. These limitations hinder our ability to assess whether agents can meaningfully adapt to individuals across dynamic, longitudinal interactions. In this <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perspective</em> paper, we propose a conceptual lens for rethinking evaluation in adaptive personalization — shifting the focus from static performance snapshots to interaction-aware, evolving assessments. We organize this lens around three core components: (1) persona-based user simulation with temporally evolving preference models; (2) structured elicitation protocols inspired by reference interviews to extract preferences in context; and (3) adaptation-aware evaluation mechanisms that measure how agent behavior improves across sessions and tasks. While recent works have embraced LLM-driven user simulation, we situate this practice within a broader paradigm for evaluating agents over time. To illustrate our ideas, we conduct a case study in e-commerce search using the PersonalWAB dataset. Beyond presenting a framework, our work lays a conceptual foundation for understanding and evaluating personalization as a continuous, user-centric endeavor.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Personalized agents,
Adaptive information retrieval,
User modeling,
Simulated users,
Conceptual framework,
Dynamic evaluation
</div><span id="id16" class="ltx_note ltx_role_cc-license"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">cc-license: </span>by-nc-sa</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Information Retrieval (IR) systems have traditionally relied on static benchmarks and fixed evaluation methodologies to assess performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kelly et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. Rooted in the Cranfield paradigm, these approaches emphasized document relevance judged by the same individuals who expressed the information need <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cleverdon, 1960</a>)</cite>. Over time, practical constraints led to the decoupling of need expression and relevance judgment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scholer et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, and recent developments such as LLM-as-a-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> have further shifted evaluation away from human users altogether.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While these adaptations offer scalability, they move evaluation even further from real-world personalization needs. Traditional methods fail to capture how users’ goals evolve over time, and thus struggle to assess an agent’s ability to adapt. This is especially limiting in the context of modern AI-driven agents — including recommender systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaur et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and conversational interfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> — which increasingly tailor responses in real-time using signals from ongoing interaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate this shift, Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares several evaluation paradigms, highlighting a progression from static human assessments to more dynamic, simulation-based alternatives. While recent work uses LLMs for both need expression and relevance judgments, we propose an integrated, task-grounded evaluation paradigm where simulated personas engage in iterative interactions, enabling the assessment of an agent’s ability to model and adapt to evolving user preferences over time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of different evaluation paradigms in IR. While recent works also use LLMs for both need expression and relevance judgment, our framework integrates these roles in a task-grounded, adaptive evaluation loop.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S1.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:40pt;vertical-align:-18.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-43.0pt,5.0pt) scale(0.800474380994087,0.800474380994087) ;">
<table id="S1.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Category</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cranfield</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-Cranfield</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM as a Judge</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.5.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our Framework</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Information Need</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Human A</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Human A</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Human A</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">AI Persona A</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Relevance Judgment</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Human A</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Human B</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">LLM</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">AI Persona A</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Personalization</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">No</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">No</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">No</td>
<td id="S1.T1.5.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Yes</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perspective</em> paper, we propose a conceptual foundation for evaluating personalized agents through dynamic, simulated interactions. Drawing inspiration from the reference interview model in information seeking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dervin and Dewdney, 1986</a>)</cite>, we envision agents engaging in structured dialogues with evolving user personas — refining their understanding of preferences over time. Our proposal centers on three components: (1) simulated personas with temporally evolving preferences; (2) structured user-agent interactions for eliciting preferences; and (3) longitudinal feedback loops that support dynamic assessment of adaptation. Unlike traditional methods, which
rely on static benchmarks, we incorporate real-time user feedback
via LLM-based simulation for a nuanced dynamic assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by presenting the evolution of personalization in information retrieval tasks in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Personalization ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, highlighting how user modeling has shifted from static profiles to more context-aware, temporally grounded paradigms. Building on this foundation, Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Dynamic Evaluation ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> draws from emerging literature to argue for a dynamic evaluation framework that can better capture the fluid and adaptive nature of user preferences in real-world interactions. To operationalize this vision, Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> introduces a structured methodology centered around multi-turn, cross-session personalization, demonstrated through an online shopping scenario instantiated using the PersonalWAB benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, our findings reveal that even when personalized agents successfully integrate prior feedback across sessions, such feedback may be misaligned with the user’s evolving context or intent, leading to overly rigid or misplaced personalization. This observation opens up research direction: the need for selective and context-sensitive incorporation of historical signals. We posit that evaluating personalized systems requires moving beyond static relevance metrics and instead embracing proactive misalignment detection, temporal adaptability, and goal-aware feedback interpretation as core dimensions. Through this paper, we aim to foreground the limitations of current personalization paradigms and establish a new evaluative lens for assessing the evolving needs of users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Personalization</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IR systems have traditionally struggled to keep pace with the evolving nature of user information needs. While personalization emerged as a response to this challenge, most existing strategies still rely on static or coarse approximations of user behavior. This section traces the evolution of personalization from early IR models to contemporary generative agents and highlights the need for a more dynamic, context-sensitive approach.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Personalization in Traditional IR Systems.</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early IR systems, based on keyword matching and Boolean logic, often returned an overwhelming number of results, many of which lacked relevance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlson, 2003</a>)</cite>. These systems failed to incorporate contextual cues or model user intent, prompting the development of personalization techniques designed to tailor retrieval outcomes to individual users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harman, 1992</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several methodologies were introduced to support this personalization goal. <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">User profiling</em> generated user models from historical queries and click data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bennett et al., 2012</a>)</cite>. <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Collaborative filtering</em> inferred preferences from similar users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koren et al., 2009</a>)</cite>, while <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">content-based filtering</em> recommended items resembling previously consumed content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarwar et al., 2001</a>)</cite>. <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Contextual modeling</em> added features such as time, location, or device into the retrieval pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lops et al., 2011</a>)</cite>. Collectively, these strategies improved relevance but treated user behavior as largely static. As a result, they were insufficient for capturing the rapid or session-level shifts in user preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sloan and Wang, 2015</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To model evolving behavior, researchers began incorporating adaptive user modeling techniques based on sequential decision-making. Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) allowed systems to update predictions based on short and long-term interaction histories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shani et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janarthanam and Lemon, 2014</a>)</cite>. More recent models, such as CoPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, leverage large language models (LLMs) and cognitive memory to represent dynamic user preferences across sessions. However, many of these techniques remain reactive and are limited by assumptions of smooth or predictable preference evolution. This restricts their applicability in open-ended or conversational settings, where users often shift goals fluidly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Generative Personalized Agents for IR</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The advent of LLMs has enabled a new class of personalized agents that can engage users in naturalistic, multi-turn dialogue. These agents can generate contextualized responses by drawing on a rich history of user interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radlinski and Craswell, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dalton et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, reformulate queries on the fly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaur et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and tailor recommendations based on subtle cues extracted from conversation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baek et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salemi et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Additionally, retrieval-augmented and multimodal systems allow agents to ground their responses in structured databases or cross-domain signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salemi et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, there has been limited
development of frameworks that allow us to assess adaptation to changing behaviors of generative models. Most current evaluations still depend on static datasets and fixed metrics like click-through rate or response relevance. These fail to capture whether an agent truly understands and adapts to a user’s evolving goals. Reinforcement learning approaches, while promising, often assume simplified feedback signals and overlook long-term preference shifts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shani et al., 2005</a>)</cite>. Even sophisticated models like RAG and dynamic memory networks rarely assess multi-session personalization effectiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Efforts to model user behavior dynamically include theoretical frameworks such as Dynamic Information Retrieval (DIR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sloan and Wang, 2015</a>)</cite>, which models how retrieval actions influence future relevance. However, DIR is largely system-centric and lacks user-facing modeling components. Similarly, MDP frameworks rely on fixed state-transition assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shani et al., 2005</a>)</cite>, limiting their flexibility in natural, open-domain dialogue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, the research community has increasingly turned to user simulation. Zhai and Balog <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai and Balog, 2023</a>)</cite> provide a comprehensive survey of LLM-based user simulation methods, showing how these models can mimic realistic user behavior for training and evaluation. However, most existing uses of user simulation are limited to query generation or pointwise feedback. Few offer longitudinal, task-grounded simulations that reflect how users evolve across sessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper builds on these insights by embedding simulated users into realistic tasks and evaluating agents over time. We combine preference elicitation, goal modeling, and iterative feedback within one evaluation loop. This enables better assessment of how well agents adapt not just within, but across multiple user sessions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Dynamic Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional metrics such as precision, recall, mean reciprocal rank (MRR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>)</cite>, mean average precision (MAP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baeza-Yates and Ribeiro-Neto, 1999</a>)</cite>, and normalized discounted cumulative gain (NDCG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>)</cite> have long served as standard tools for evaluating personalized IR systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees, 1999</a>)</cite>. While these metrics offer robust insights into ranking quality for static queries and profiles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, they fall short in capturing the temporal dynamics inherent in modern user-agent interactions. Specifically, they do not account for evolving user intent, preference shifts, or the iterative and contextualized nature of real-world personalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tabrizi et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents, on the other hand, operate not as one-shot recommenders but as persistent, adaptive agents that evolve in tandem with the user across extended periods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radlinski and Craswell, 2017</a>)</cite>. Their behavior reflects a continuous process of preference refinement, goal realignment, and contextual adaptation. As a result, static evaluation frameworks are ill-equipped to assess the quality of such longitudinal alignment. In particular, traditional methods struggle to capture phenomena like preference drift, response consistency, feedback incorporation, and user modeling over multi-turn or multi-session interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This calls for a fundamental rethinking of evaluation design. To rigorously assess adaptive personalization, we must move beyond static performance snapshots and embrace dynamic, interaction-aware evaluation methodologies. Such frameworks should assess whether agents can generalize from prior context, retain and update preferences, and deliver value that compounds over time. Although directly measuring internal user model fidelity is inherently difficult, a suite of proxy signals — such as topical relevance, diversity of suggestions, and novelty of recommendations — can serve as effective stand-ins for adaptive competence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a methodology that operationalizes these evaluation desiderata. By combining user simulation, structured preference elicitation, and longitudinal session modeling, our framework offers a principled approach for quantifying how well personalized agents adapt to user needs in evolving, task-oriented environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel1">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03984v1/figures/female_personaa1.png" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/703;" width="476" height="703" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Female Persona</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03984v1/figures/male_persona.png" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/712;" width="476" height="712" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Male Persona</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of two distinct user personas used in our evaluation. Persona A is a graduate student with lifestyle- and wellness-oriented preferences; Persona B is a retired user focused on quality, value, and trusted brands.</span><span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">This image illustrates two distinct personas used in marketing strategies. The female persona, depicted on the left in a purple box, includes attributes such as age, occupation, price sensitivity, shopping interests, brand preferences, diversity preference, interaction complexity, tone and style, item reference, and focus aspect. The male persona, shown on the right in a beige box, features similar attributes tailored to a different demographic, highlighting differences in shopping interests, brand preferences, and other characteristics.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To enable robust evaluation of adaptive agents, we propose a framework centered around three broad pillars: (1) Simulated Users and Agent, (2) Task-Grounded Interaction and Elicitation, and (3) Dynamic Output Evaluation. These encompass a set of operational components that together capture how well a personalized agent aligns with user preferences over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulated Users and Agents:</span> This dimension focuses on modeling the user and the personalized system under evaluation.</p>
<ol id="S3.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Simulated Users (Personas)</span>: Virtual users with evolving preferences across interactions. These personas help instantiate realistic behavioral shifts over time.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Personalized Agent</span>: The system being evaluated, which must dynamically adjust recommendations based on accumulated interaction data.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Grounded Interaction and Elicitation:</span> This dimension anchors the evaluation in realistic goals and structured interactions.</p>
<ol id="S3.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Based Interactions (Work Tasks)</span>: Real-world inspired tasks (e.g., shopping, planning) that provide context for agent-user interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braggaar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Personalization Elicitation (Reference Interview)</span>: Structured methods (e.g., guided Q&amp;A, think-aloud protocols) used to extract user preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ericsson and Simon, 1984</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset</span>: The item corpus used for retrieval and ranking, offering the space in which personalization is tested.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Output Evaluation:</span> This dimension captures how well the system’s outputs reflect and adapt to user preferences over time.</p>
<ol id="S3.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ranked Items</span>: The retrieved outputs ranked based on inferred user preferences and interaction history.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Evaluation and Measurements</span>: An iterative evaluation process using simulated feedback on metrics such as relevance, diversity, and novelty.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We next demonstrate this evaluation framework through a case study in online shopping using the PersonalWAB benchmark.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel2">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03984v1/figures/female_query1.png" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/185;" width="476" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query by Female User</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03984v1/figures/male_query1.png" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/185;" width="476" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query by Male User</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Sample queries by two users in session 1. </span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">This image illustrates two queries in session 1 by distinct personas. The female persona, depicted on the left in a purple box, is querying for eco-friendly cleaning options. The male persona, shown on the right in a beige box, is querying for high-quality PC-cable extensions or similar accessories.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ground our conceptual framework in a concrete setting, we present a case study in the domain of online shopping. This demonstration illustrates how the proposed evaluation paradigm, centered on simulated users, task-grounded interactions, and dynamic feedback, can be instantiated in practice. While our theoretical model is domain-agnostic, the shopping scenario offers a familiar and richly contextualized environment for assessing adaptive personalization over multiple sessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the PersonalWAB benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to simulate user-agent interactions and to evaluate how personalized agents adapt to evolving user preferences across sessions. PersonalWAB offers a uniquely rich foundation for this task and reflects varied goal-oriented behaviors, allowing us to simulate both short-term needs (e.g., one-off purchases) and longer-term preference patterns (e.g., brand loyalty or lifestyle alignment). This makes PersonalWAB particularly well-suited for instantiating adaptive, multi-turn interactions. The components introduced in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Dynamic Evaluation ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (simulated personas, structured elicitation, and iterative evaluation) are operationalized here through a multi-stage simulation setup involving representative users and compound shopping subtasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Work Task: Shopping Assistance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conceptualization.</span> We demonstrate our evaluation in an online shopping scenario, where the overall work task is assisting users in making informed purchase decisions. We define three key subtasks within this broader goal: product discovery, price comparison, and personalized recommendations. These subtasks represent distinct yet interconnected activities that agents must effectively support to fulfill the end-to-end shopping experience.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We simulate two distinct user sessions—<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>—to evaluate temporal adaptation. In <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span>, the agent helps the user complete an initial shopping task by guiding product exploration, comparing prices across listings, and recommending options tailored to elicited preferences. These preferences may be derived either implicitly (via user actions) or explicitly (via structured interviews) and inform the agent to form a behavioral profile reflective of user needs, values, and product inclinations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span> simulates a return visit where the user seeks follow-up or complementary products. Here, the agent leverages the context from <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span> to personalize assistance: suggesting compatible items, estimating budget preferences, and refining recommendations to reflect continuity in the user’s journey. This setup allows us to evaluate the agent’s ability to incorporate prior user interactions into present decision-making;
refine recommendations over time based on evolving preferences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Interview between Agent and Simulated User</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Task description <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, Sub-task description <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, Dataset description <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, Agent <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, Simulated User <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, Number of turns <math id="alg1.l0.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             List of QA utterances <span id="alg1.l0a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">QA_utterances</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Initialize <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{QA\_utterances}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{QA\_utterances}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leftarrow A.\text{generate\_question}(T,S,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>generate_question</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leftarrow A.\text{generate\_question}(T,S,D)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Agent’s question}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leftarrow U.\text{respond\_to\_question}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>respond_to_question</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leftarrow U.\text{respond\_to\_question}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Simulated user responds}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{QA\_utterances}.\text{append}((q,r))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>append</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{QA\_utterances}.\text{append}((q,r))</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Store the pair}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">QA_utterances</span>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Setting.</span> We now discuss our empirical choices made to instantiate simulated users, define realistic shopping tasks, structure multi-session interactions, and operationalize preference elicitation and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Dataset and User Profiles</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PersonalWAB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> builds upon the Amazon Review corpus and includes user behavior traces—such as purchases and product ratings—across five product domains: <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Electronics</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Home and Kitchen</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Grocery and Gourmet Food</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Clothing, Shoes and Jewelry</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Health and Household</span>. Each user profile contains rich metadata, including:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Basic attributes:</em> gender, age, and occupation,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Shopping preferences:</em> brand affinities, price sensitivity, and interests,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Behavioral traits:</em> diversity-seeking, interaction complexity, and review awareness.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since occupation is a key user attribute for downstream tasks, we filtered out profiles with missing occupation data. This yielded 377 female and 67 male users. We then selected a balanced and representative subset comprising 60 female and 60 male users (<math id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=120" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=120</annotation></semantics></math>) via stratified random sampling to ensure diversity across demographic and behavioral dimensions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Retrieval and Ranking of Items for <span id="alg2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span></figcaption>
<div id="alg2.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:156.1pt;vertical-align:-75.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-18.9pt,9.5pt) scale(0.891381527715532,0.891381527715532) ;">
<div id="alg2.6.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_listing" style="width:345.0pt;">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 Task description <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, Sub-task description <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, Dataset description <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, Agent <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, Simulated User <math id="alg2.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, Reference Interviews <span id="alg2.l0.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">QA_utterances</span>, Dataset <math id="alg2.l0.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 Ranked list of items for Session A <span id="alg2.l0a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_A</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 Initialize <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{retrieved\_items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>retrieved_items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_retrieve</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{retrieved\_items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Retrieve items from dataset <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items\_A},\text{QA\_utterances})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>retrieved_items_A</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items\_A},\text{QA\_utterances})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Rank retrieved items for <span id="alg2.l3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>aggregate_and_finalize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Ranked_Items_A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_A}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_A})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Aggregate and finalize the rankings for <span id="alg2.l4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <span id="alg2.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_A</span>

</div>
</div>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Illustrative Personas</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To make the evaluation concrete, we focus on two representative user personas (Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Dynamic Evaluation ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) for detailed analysis throughout this paper. However, we present broader statistics on overall preprocessed data in later sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We selected two contrasting personas to reflect diversity in age, shopping goals, and interaction styles. <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persona A</span> is a female who is a graduate student (25–34 years) and shops across lifestyle-oriented categories such as kitchen appliances, wellness, home goods, coffee, and clothing, with an enthusiastic and practical tone. In contrast, <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persona B</span> is a retired man (56+ years old), focused on health supplements, electronics, household essentials, and snacks, communicating in a detail-rich and brand-aware manner. These divergent personas—s— ranging from lifestyle-driven choices to performance- and reliability-focused decisions—allow us to assess whether the agent’s adaptability to users with distinct needs and patterns.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel3">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBDEEB;" fill="#EBDEEB" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P1]Looking for a high-quality skincare product to soothe rosacea and reduce redness. Prefer something with great reviews and proven effectiveness. Any recommendations?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="55.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 55.23" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,55.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 49.69 C 0 52.75 2.48 55.23 5.53 55.23 L 471.84 55.23 C 474.9 55.23 477.38 52.75 477.38 49.69 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 49.69 C 0 52.75 2.48 55.23 5.53 55.23 L 471.84 55.23 C 474.9 55.23 477.38 52.75 477.38 49.69 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="41.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 39.94)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-robot" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Thanks for sharing! To help you better, could you tell me if you have a preferred budget range for skincare products? Also, are there specific brands you trust or have tried before?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBDEEB;" fill="#EBDEEB" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P1]:I’d like to keep it under <math id="S4.F3.sf1.pic3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>. I’ve heard of brands like CeraVe and La Roche-Posay from friends but haven’t used them myself. I’m open to trying others too.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic4" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="88.44" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 88.44" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,88.44) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 82.9 C 0 85.96 2.48 88.44 5.53 88.44 L 471.84 88.44 C 474.9 88.44 477.38 85.96 477.38 82.9 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 82.9 C 0 85.96 2.48 88.44 5.53 88.44 L 471.84 88.44 C 474.9 88.44 477.38 85.96 477.38 82.9 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:5.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="75.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 73.14)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-robot" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic4.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Got it! Both CeraVe and La Roche-Posay are highly rated for sensitive skin and could be a great fit. Just to clarify:
Are you looking for a daily moisturizer, a treatment serum, or something else specific?
How important is it that the product is fragrance-free or dermatologist-recommended?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic5" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="36.6" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 36.6" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,36.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 28.3 C 0 32.89 3.72 36.6 8.3 36.6 L 469.07 36.6 C 473.66 36.6 477.38 32.89 477.38 28.3 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBDEEB;" fill="#EBDEEB" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 28.3 C 0 32.89 3.72 36.6 8.3 36.6 L 469.07 36.6 C 473.66 36.6 477.38 32.89 477.38 28.3 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 9.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:1.5em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="23.2" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 20.77)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic5.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.pic5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P1]: I’m mostly looking for a calming, fragrance-free daily moisturizer.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.sf1.pic6" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="38.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 38.62" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,38.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 33.09 C 0 36.14 2.48 38.62 5.53 38.62 L 471.84 38.62 C 474.9 38.62 477.38 36.14 477.38 33.09 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 33.09 C 0 36.14 2.48 38.62 5.53 38.62 L 471.84 38.62 C 474.9 38.62 477.38 36.14 477.38 33.09 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:1.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 23.33)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
