</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Structured reference interviews for two distinct personas. The agent adapts its elicitation strategy to each user’s tone, goals, and preferences.</span><span id="acmlabel3" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">none</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Simulation Setup</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simulate interactions between user personas and a personalized agent using the AutoGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> framework, integrated with GPT-4o-mini. Although AutoGen serves as our simulation backbone, the evaluation framework is simulation-agnostic— alternative strategies such as rule-based personas, few-shot prompting, or behaviorally cloned agents can be used with minimal modification. We chose AutoGen for its support of persistent persona memory, modular agent roles, and realistic multi-turn dialogue, which are critical for studying adaptation over time. Our contribution lies not in the simulator itself, but in how it enables a structured evaluation loop comprising preference elicitation, multi-session grounding, and LLM-based feedback—key components of dynamic personalization assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each persona-based user simulation interacts with the agent across two temporally distinct shopping sessions and in both, the agent supports three subtasks of product discovery, price comparison, and personalized recommendation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span> begins with a goal-directed user query (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Dynamic Evaluation ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Persona A requests a high-quality skincare product to soothe rosacea, prioritizing strong reviews and proven effectiveness. Persona B seeks reliable digital storage for photos and videos, asking for high-capacity, fast memory cards or external drives. These queries initiate a structured reference interview through which the agent elicits key preferences—such as brand, price, and product features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS1.SSSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>, the user returns for a related task. The agent is expected to incorporate previously inferred preferences while adapting to new needs. This session-based structure allows us to evaluate whether the agent retains user context, generalizes across tasks, and improves recommendation relevance over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Step 1: Reference Interview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conceptualization</span>: The reference interview serves as the initial elicitation step, where the agent gathers key user preferences— such as brand inclinations, price range, feature priorities, and urgency— through a structured, multi-turn dialogue. This interaction is designed to approximate realistic information-seeking behavior while enabling controlled evaluation of the agent’s ability to extract actionable intent. Unlike conventional recommender systems that rely on continual real-time feedback, we restrict the agent to a single elicitation session per task. This constraint reflects real-world scenarios where users may be unavailable for persistent feedback, and it allows us to isolate the agent’s elicitation competence without confounding adaptation effects. The agent conducts a single five-turn reference interview per session to elicit key preferences, using the full dialogue to generate a retrieval query without post-retrieval refinement. Moreover, this one-shot setup ensures reproducibility and provides a stable foundation for downstream recommendation and evaluation. The agent uses templated but adaptive prompts to conduct the interview (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Dataset and User Profiles ‣ 4.1. Work Task: Shopping Assistance ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), balancing generalizability with contextual grounding based on each user’s query and persona.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Demonstration: </span>We simulate user-agent dialogues via two modular agents: a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User Agent</span> and a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shopping Assistant Agent</span>, each instantiated with separate system prompts and role-specific behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The User Agent is simulated via a persona-grounded prompt that encodes demographic and behavioral attributes extracted from the PersonalWAB dataset. As shown below, the template instructs the model to stay in character as a shopper with specific preferences, tone, and interests. It encourages the user to express goals, ask questions, and share personal constraints, while explicitly avoiding assistant-like behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="277.54" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 277.54" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,277.54) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.32 L 0 274.22 C 0 276.06 1.49 277.54 3.32 277.54 L 474.06 277.54 C 475.89 277.54 477.38 276.06 477.38 274.22 L 477.38 3.32 C 477.38 1.49 475.89 0 474.06 0 L 3.32 0 C 1.49 0 0 1.49 0 3.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 3.32 L 0.55 256.26 L 476.82 256.26 L 476.82 3.32 C 476.82 1.79 475.58 0.55 474.06 0.55 L 3.32 0.55 C 1.79 0.55 0.55 1.79 0.55 3.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 263.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:28.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="397.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:28.74em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">User Simulation Prompt Template</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 9.41)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.88em;--ltx-fo-height:17.36em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="242.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 240.15)" width="537.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.88em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a shopper looking to purchase an item. Stay in character as a <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{age}</span>-year-old <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{gender}</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{occupation}</span>. Your goal is to find the best option based on your preferences,<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{price sensitivity}</span> , and <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{shopping interests}</span>.</span></span>
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">When responding:
- Provide information about your preferences, interests, and constraints.
- Ask questions about product details, discounts, or comparisons.
- Express opinions and react naturally to recommendations.
- Do <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not</span> provide recommendations or act like an assistant.
- Keep your tone <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{tone and style}</span>.
- If unsure, ask for clarification rather than making recommendations.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Agent, in turn, is prompted using a structured reference interview template (shown below), which guides it to elicit relevant user preferences for the three shopping subtasks: product discovery, price comparison, and personalized recommendations. Conversations are capped at a maximum of 8 turns (4 per agent), balancing richness with efficiency. Both agents are run with a temperature of 0.7 to allow for natural variation in phrasing while maintaining role consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Each simulated session begins with a user query (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Dynamic Evaluation ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which then initiates a multi-turn interaction. The agent adaptively asks clarifying questions to elicit key shopping constraints, such as brand familiarity, price range, product features, urgency, and user priorities, within a single-session scope. Unlike recommender systems that rely on continuous user feedback, our framework focuses on single-session elicitation to enable controlled, reproducible evaluation of personalization strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Illustrative Personas ‣ 4.1. Work Task: Shopping Assistance ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents structured reference interviews for two contrasting user personas. Persona A (<span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Figure <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Illustrative Personas ‣ 4.1. Work Task: Shopping Assistance ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a></span>), a college-age student, expresses interest in skincare products for rosacea. The agent probes for budget, product type, and brand familiarity, quickly converging on the user’s preference for fragrance-free, calming moisturizers under $30. The agent’s ability to align its questioning with the user’s tone and constraints demonstrates sensitivity to lifestyle-focused, peer-reviewed decision signals.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="239.57" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 239.57" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,239.57) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 234.03 C 0 237.09 2.48 239.57 5.53 239.57 L 471.84 239.57 C 474.9 239.57 477.38 237.09 477.38 234.03 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 234.03 C 0 237.09 2.48 239.57 5.53 239.57 L 471.84 239.57 C 474.9 239.57 477.38 237.09 477.38 234.03 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:16.21em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="226.16" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 224.28)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Great! Based on your preferences for a calming, fragrance-free moisturizer under $30 for rosacea-prone skin, here are a few options to consider:<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Aquaphor Healing Ointment Advanced Therapy Skin Protectant<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $5.49</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Fragrance-free, dermatologist-recommended, and ideal for dry, sensitive, or rosacea-prone skin. Its water-free formulation supports barrier repair and redness relief.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Nature’s Bounty Vitamin E Oil, 30,000IU<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $8.95</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">A topical antioxidant oil that hydrates and calms irritated skin. Fragrance-free and suitable for sensitive skincare routines.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Puriya Eczema Cream (NEA-accepted)<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $32.21</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Designed for sensitive, inflamed skin. Plant-based and fragrance-free, featuring shea butter and hyaluronic acid. Slightly above budget but clinically backed.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona A (Female)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="256.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 256.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,256.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:17.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="243.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 241.42)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Absolutely! Based on your need for reliable, high-capacity storage under $100, here are three strong candidates:
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Toshiba Canvio Slim II 1TB Portable Hard Drive<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $101.00</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">A slim, lightweight drive with 1TB capacity, ideal for storing large volumes of high-resolution photos and videos. Offers encryption, automatic backup, and cross-platform compatibility.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Seagate FreeAgent Desktop 250GB Hard Drive<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $64.99</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">While the capacity is modest, this drive offers dependable performance, quiet operation, and simple plug-and-play USB 2.0 connectivity. It’s an excellent archival drive for long-term storage of curated media collections.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Toshiba 320GB USB 2.0 Portable External HDD<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $60.00</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">A practical and portable drive with shock protection and ramp loading technology, designed to safely transport and back up media content.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona B (Male)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Final product recommendations for two distinct personas. The agent integrates interview context to tailor suggestions aligned with user goals, budget, and preferences.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="244.34" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 244.34" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,244.34) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.32 L 0 241.01 C 0 242.85 1.49 244.34 3.32 244.34 L 474.06 244.34 C 475.89 244.34 477.38 242.85 477.38 241.01 L 477.38 3.32 C 477.38 1.49 475.89 0 474.06 0 L 3.32 0 C 1.49 0 0 1.49 0 3.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 3.32 L 0.55 223.05 L 476.82 223.05 L 476.82 3.32 C 476.82 1.79 475.58 0.55 474.06 0.55 L 3.32 0.55 C 1.79 0.55 0.55 1.79 0.55 3.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 230.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:28.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="397.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:28.74em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Shopping Assistant Prompt Template</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 9.41)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.88em;--ltx-fo-height:14.96em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="209.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 206.94)" width="537.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.88em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a highly capable shopping assistant designed to help users with their shopping needs. Your primary work task is to assist users in their shopping journey by handling three subtasks: (1) <span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Product Discovery</span> — identify and suggest relevant products based on user preferences; (2) <span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Price Comparison</span> — provide competitive price analysis across multiple vendors; and (3) <span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Personalized Recommendations</span> — tailor suggestions based on user history, preferences, and context.
To achieve these tasks, you must conduct a structured reference interview to elicit user needs, including their preferences for these three sub-tasks such as preferred brands, price range, product features, shopping urgency, and other relevant factors. Your goal is to efficiently gather the user’s needs, requirements, and preferences without overwhelming the user.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Persona B (<span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fig. <a href="#S4.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Illustrative Personas ‣ 4.1. Work Task: Shopping Assistance ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a></span>), a retired male user, seeks high-capacity storage solutions for digital media. The agent tailors its questions to technical specifications such as capacity (<math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1TB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1TB</annotation></semantics></math>), device type (external drives), and brand reliability. The user’s responses highlight emphasis on performance, practicality, and value, and the agent effectively narrows the product space to familiar brands like SanDisk and Toshiba.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">These two interviews highlight distinct elicitation pathways: one focused on lifestyle cues and social proof (Persona A), and the other grounded in technical performance and brand trust (Persona B). The agent adapts to each user’s tone, vocabulary, and decision criteria, showcasing its capacity to extract meaningful personalization signals through targeted, session-bounded dialogue. This contrast highlights not just the breadth of personalization signals across users, but also the importance of context-sensitive dialogue structuring. By tailoring its inquiry framework to each user’s mental model, the agent demonstrates early-stage proactive reasoning—an essential step toward evaluating true adaptability in personalized systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Step 2: Item Recommendation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second step in our evaluation framework assesses the agent’s ability to retrieve and rank products based on the preferences elicited during the reference interview (Step 1). While this step includes standard ranking and retrieval components, our focus extends beyond traditional recommendation paradigms. Instead of relying on static profiles or collaborative filtering, we evaluate the agent’s capacity for dynamic personalization, specifically, its ability to (1) interpret multi-turn, elicited preferences to generate relevant and diverse recommendations, (2) balance personalization with exploration by incorporating both direct user cues and novel suggestions, and (3) adapt over time by refining recommendations in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span> based on prior interactions in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span>. In doing so, this step operationalizes item recommendation not as a one-shot matching task, but as an evolving, context-sensitive challenge that reflects real-world user behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Retrieval and Ranking of Items for <span id="alg3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span></figcaption>
<div id="alg3.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:209.6pt;vertical-align:-102.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-18.9pt,12.8pt) scale(0.891381527715532,0.891381527715532) ;">
