               Ranked list of items for Session B <span id="alg3.l0a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_B</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
               Initialize <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{retrieved\_items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>retrieved_items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_retrieve</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{retrieved\_items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Retrieve items for <span id="alg3.l2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span> from dataset <math id="alg3.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items\_B},\text{QA\_utterances})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>retrieved_items_B</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>QA_utterances</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items\_B},\text{QA\_utterances})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Rank retrieved items for <span id="alg3.l3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>aggregate_and_finalize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_B})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Aggregate and finalize the rankings for <span id="alg3.l4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg3.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <span id="alg3.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_B</span>

</div>
</div>
</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Challenge 1: On-Task Item Recommendation for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here the agent is assessed if it can effectively translate the preferences elicited during the reference interview into a ranked list of relevant products. Unlike traditional recommenders that rely on collaborative filtering or static profiles, this setting tests the agent’s ability to construct intent-aware queries and return on-topic results from a cold start. The underlying logic is outlined in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Dataset and User Profiles ‣ 4.1. Work Task: Shopping Assistance ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F5.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBDEEB;" fill="#EBDEEB" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P1]: Can you help me find a tinted moisturizer that gives light coverage and looks natural throughout the day?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="239.57" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 239.57" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,239.57) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 234.03 C 0 237.09 2.48 239.57 5.53 239.57 L 471.84 239.57 C 474.9 239.57 477.38 237.09 477.38 234.03 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 234.03 C 0 237.09 2.48 239.57 5.53 239.57 L 471.84 239.57 C 474.9 239.57 477.38 237.09 477.38 234.03 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:16.21em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="226.16" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 224.28)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sure! Based on your earlier preferences for gentle skincare for rosacea-prone skin and your current interest in light, natural coverage, here are three great options:<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Aquaphor Healing Ointment Advanced Therapy Skin Protectant<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $5.49</span> — A dermatologist-recommended base that soothes dry, irritated skin. Can be layered beneath lightweight tinted moisturizers for barrier support.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. CeraVe Healing Ointment (12 oz)<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $19.28</span>- Fragrance-free and NEA-approved. Provides all-day hydration and barrier repair without interfering with natural makeup finish.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Calmoseptine Ointment (4 oz)<span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.pic2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $7.22</span> - A multipurpose skin protectant that reduces redness and irritation, making it a good prep step for natural-tint skin applications.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona A (Female)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F5.sf2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#DCEBFA;" fill="#DCEBFA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P2]: Also curious—do you know any high-quality magnesium supplements that help with leg cramps and sleep?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="256.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 256.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,256.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:17.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="243.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 241.42)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Absolutely! Building on your interest in supplements and trusted brands like Doctor’s Best and Nature Made, here are a few high-quality options for leg cramps and restful sleep:<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Doctor’s Best Brain Magnesium (90 Count)<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $39.29</span> — Features Magtein (Magnesium L-Threonate), shown to improve sleep, memory, and stress relief. High absorption and brain-targeted.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Vital Vitamins Brain Booster<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $21.95</span> — Combines B12, Ginkgo, and DMAE for stress and cognitive support without caffeine—great for retired users prioritizing natural sleep aids.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. SYNERLOGIC Chrome OS Keyboard Shortcut Sticker<span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.pic2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $4.99</span> — Given your interest in electronics, this is a smart add-on for productivity and digital confidence—especially if using Chromebooks.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona B (Male)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Session B recommendations for two distinct personas. The agent integrates current queries with prior interview context to offer goal-aligned, personalized suggestions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Analysis:</span> To assess the agent’s capability in generating on-task recommendations in Session A, we analyze its outputs across multiple personas with varied goals. The agent was prompted with a structured reference interview and user profile, from which it generated a concise query phrase and ranked a set of retrieved items. The generated phrase was then used to query a multi-field product index composed of structured attributes (e.g., title, features, category). This initial retrieval helped narrow down candidate items aligned with user intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="183.45" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 183.45" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,183.45) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.32 L 0 180.13 C 0 181.97 1.49 183.45 3.32 183.45 L 474.06 183.45 C 475.89 183.45 477.38 181.97 477.38 180.13 L 477.38 3.32 C 477.38 1.49 475.89 0 474.06 0 L 3.32 0 C 1.49 0 0 1.49 0 3.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 3.32 L 0.55 162.17 L 476.82 162.17 L 476.82 3.32 C 476.82 1.79 475.58 0.55 474.06 0.55 L 3.32 0.55 C 1.79 0.55 0.55 1.79 0.55 3.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 169.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:28.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="397.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:28.74em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Query Formulation Prompt for Recommendation</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 12.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.88em;--ltx-fo-height:10.16em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="142.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 140.52)" width="537.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.88em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a shopping assistant tasked with ranking products for a specific user. Based on the following structured profile and reference interview, generate a concise search phrase (under 5 words) that captures the user’s needs.
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Profile:</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{user_profile}</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference Interview:</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{reference_interview}</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Output:<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> One short phrase that reflects the user’s intent.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To further personalize results, the agent was tasked with re-ranking the retrieved products based on both relevance and intra-list diversity, using the following instruction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="233.27" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 233.27" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,233.27) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.32 L 0 229.94 C 0 231.78 1.49 233.27 3.32 233.27 L 474.06 233.27 C 475.89 233.27 477.38 231.78 477.38 229.94 L 477.38 3.32 C 477.38 1.49 475.89 0 474.06 0 L 3.32 0 C 1.49 0 0 1.49 0 3.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 3.32 L 0.55 211.99 L 476.82 211.99 L 476.82 3.32 C 476.82 1.79 475.58 0.55 474.06 0.55 L 3.32 0.55 C 1.79 0.55 0.55 1.79 0.55 3.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 219.17)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:28.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="397.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:28.74em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">User Simulation Prompt Template</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 12.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.88em;--ltx-fo-height:13.76em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="192.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 190.34)" width="537.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.88em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a helpful agent that has retrieved a set of candidate products for a specific user. Based on the user’s profile and interview, re-rank the items below by considering both preference alignment and diversity. Provide a ranked list of the top 3 products along with a one-line justification for each.</span></span>
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User Profile:<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{user_profile}</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Reference Interview:<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{reference_interview}</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Retrieved Items:<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{retrieved_items}</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Output:<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Top 3 ranked items with justifications.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative Analysis:</span> Figures <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2. Step 1: Reference Interview ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2. Step 1: Reference Interview ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> present contrasting examples of on-task recommendation performance. For Persona A, who seeks a fragrance-free moisturizer for rosacea-prone skin, the agent surfaces medically appropriate and semantically aligned products (e.g., Aquaphor, Puriya) from the correct category, balancing relevance and diversity by including both traditional and plant-based options. For Persona B, who requires high-capacity media storage, the agent integrates interview cues (e.g., trusted brands like Toshiba) and offers form factor diversity (portable vs. desktop drives), demonstrating contextual understanding of the query. Overall, these examples highlight the agent’s ability to generate semantically coherent, preference-aligned recommendations within a single session by interpreting both explicit constraints (e.g., budget, brand) and implicit values (e.g., sensitivity, usability).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Challenge 2: On-Task Item Recommendation for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This challenge builds on Challenge 1 by requiring the agent to incorporate signals from <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span>—including prior QA utterances, user feedback, and inferred preferences—when generating recommendations in <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>. The task evaluates whether the agent can preserve consistency across sessions, carry forward favored brands or product categories, and adapt recommendations dynamically to evolving user needs. <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The prompting setup closely mirrors that of Challenge 1, with additional inputs from <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span>—as detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>—appended to guide preference adaptation.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Analysis</span>: Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3.1. Challenge 1: On-Task Item Recommendation for Session A ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates how the agent incorporates prior user interactions from <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session A</span> to adapt its recommendations in <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span>. For Persona A (left), this adaptation is effective: the agent draws on earlier cues around sensitive skin and eco-conscious preferences to recommend products such as the Rubbermaid Clear Pitcher and Bamboo Kitchen Trolley, which align with the user’s interest in sustainability and small-space living. This indicates that retaining prior signals can lead to coherent, goal-aligned personalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Persona B (right), however, preference carryover is more mixed. While the agent continues to reflect performance-oriented and brand-specific signals from earlier sessions, it includes recommendations—like SSD storage devices—that are only loosely connected to the user’s new goal of building a visually clean PC setup. This suggests that when user intent shifts, unfiltered reliance on prior preferences can dilute relevance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qualitative Analysis:</span> These findings emphasize the importance of adaptive preference grounding. When user goals remain consistent, carryover supports session-level personalization. But when intent evolves, agents must selectively retain or reinterpret past signals to maintain contextual accuracy. Balancing memory and flexibility is thus essential for effective multi-session recommendation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3. </span>Challenge 3: Cross-Task Item Recommendation for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Session B</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This challenge examines whether a personalized agent can maintain continuity in preference modeling across distinct but thematically related subtasks within the same session. Unlike prior challenges that evaluated temporal adaptation across sessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such cross-task coherence is essential in real-world scenarios where users fluidly switch goals but still expect agents to retain relevant contextual grounding. Success in this challenge requires the agent not just to recall prior elicited preferences, but to abstract and reinterpret them in light of new objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The corresponding procedural flow is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ 4.3.3. Challenge 3: Cross-Task Item Recommendation for Session B ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.After completing Task A within Session B, the agent must retrieve and rank items for Task B, guided by earlier QA utterances and labeled preferences from Task A. Importantly, this flow assumes no additional elicitation—the agent must repurpose previously inferred preferences without direct feedback or clarification from the user.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span> Cross-Task Item Recommendation for Session B</figcaption>
<div id="alg4.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg4.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                
                
               Task description <math id="alg4.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, Sub-task A description <math id="alg4.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, Sub-task B description <math id="alg4.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, Dataset description <math id="alg4.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, Relevant labels from Sub-task A <span id="alg4.l0.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Relevant_Labels_A</span>, QA utterances for Sub-task A <span id="alg4.l0.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">QA_utterances_A</span>, Dataset <math id="alg4.l0.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                
                
               Ranked list of recommended items for Sub-task B <span id="alg4.l0a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_B</span>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
               Initialize <math id="alg4.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg4.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{retrieved\_items}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances\_A},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>retrieved_items</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_retrieve</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>QA_utterances_A</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{retrieved\_items}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_retrieve}(\text{QA\_utterances\_A},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg4.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Retrieve items for all QA utterances in Sub-task A}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg4.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>batch_rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>retrieved_items</mtext><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow A.\text{batch\_rank}(\text{retrieved\_items},</annotation></semantics></math>    <math id="alg4.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Relevant\_Labels\_A},\text{QA\_utterances\_B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mtext>Relevant_Labels_A</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>QA_utterances_B</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Relevant\_Labels\_A},\text{QA\_utterances\_B})</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg4.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Rank retrieved items based on Sub-task A labels and QA utterances}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg4.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>aggregate_and_finalize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Ranked_Items_B</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ranked\_Items\_B}\leftarrow\text{aggregate\_and\_finalize}(\text{Ranked\_Items\_B})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg4.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Aggregate and finalize the rankings}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg4.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg4.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <span id="alg4.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Ranked_Items_B</span>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F6.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBDEEB;" fill="#EBDEEB" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P1]: Hey there! I’m excited to explore some eco-friendly cleaning options for my kitchen. Any recommendations for effective, well-rated products that smell great?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="256.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 256.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,256.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 251.17 C 0 254.23 2.48 256.71 5.53 256.71 L 471.84 256.71 C 474.9 256.71 477.38 254.23 477.38 251.17 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:17.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="243.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 241.42)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Of course! Based on your interest in gentle, skin-safe, and natural products, here are a few highly rated, eco-conscious kitchen cleaning items that smell great and get the job done:<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Dawn Ultra Platinum Foam Dishwashing Foam (Fresh Rapids Scent)<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $3.69</span> — Absorbs 5x more grease and delivers a refreshing scent. Efficient for small-batch dishwashing with minimal water.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. OxiClean Odor Blasters Versatile Odor and Stain Remover Powder (5 lb)<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $11.78</span> — Citrus-scented and chlorine-free. Great for laundry, surfaces, and kitchen messes with eco-safe performance.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Pure Homemaker Wool Dryer Balls (6-Pack)<span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.pic2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: N/A</span> — A reusable alternative to chemical dryer sheets. Add essential oils for natural scent while softening clothes and cutting drying time.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona A (Female)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F6.sf2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="52.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 52.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,52.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#DCEBFA;" fill="#DCEBFA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 44.37 C 0 48.95 3.72 52.67 8.3 52.67 L 469.07 52.67 C 473.66 52.67 477.38 48.95 477.38 44.37 L 477.38 8.3 C 477.38 3.72 473.66 0 469.07 0 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:2.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="39.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.38)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[P2]: I’m looking for something to help with my joints—preferably a gentle supplement I can take daily.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="265.36" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 265.36" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,265.36) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 259.82 C 0 262.88 2.48 265.36 5.53 265.36 L 471.84 265.36 C 474.9 265.36 477.38 262.88 477.38 259.82 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FBE5BA;" fill="#FBE5BA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.53 L 0 259.82 C 0 262.88 2.48 265.36 5.53 265.36 L 471.84 265.36 C 474.9 265.36 477.38 262.88 477.38 259.82 L 477.38 0 L 5.53 0 C 2.48 0 0 2.48 0 5.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.06em;--ltx-fo-height:18.07em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="251.95" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 250.07)" width="581.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.06em;">
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Absolutely! Since you’ve shown interest in health supplements and ease-of-use, here are some top-rated daily joint support products tailored for comfort and effectiveness:<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1. Arazo Nutrition Glucosamine Chondroitin Turmeric MSM Boswellia (180 Tablets)<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $24.95</span> — Comprehensive joint formula supporting mobility and comfort, especially for age-related stiffness.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2. Vital Vitamins Brain Booster<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $21.95</span> — While primarily for cognition, its anti-inflammatory ingredients (DMAE, Ginkgo) may support joint-related stress relief and overall wellness.</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3. Doctor’s Best Brain Magnesium<span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.pic2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Price: $39.29</span> — Shown to ease muscle tension and support neurological function—useful for cramp and joint symptom overlap.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Persona B (Male)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cross-task recommendations in Session B. Each agent response reflects inferred needs from prior interactions in Task A to guide Task B recommendations that maintain coherence and personalization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Analysis</span>: Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3.3. Challenge 3: Cross-Task Item Recommendation for Session B ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents qualitative results for two distinct personas. For Persona A, the agent succeeds in translating previously inferred values (e.g., eco-friendliness, fragrance-free preferences, budget-consciousness) into targeted recommendations for environmentally safe kitchen cleaning products. The suggestions are not only relevant but stylistically consistent with the user’s prior interaction pattern— well-rated, and naturally formulated items.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For Persona B , the performance is more varied. While the agent correctly carries over signals such as trust in established brands and interest in daily wellness routines, some recommendations show only loose alignment with the user’s new goal. For instance, productivity accessories appear alongside joint supplements—useful perhaps in general health contexts, but peripheral to the user’s expressed intent. This suggests an overextension of prior preferences without adequate goal re-grounding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analytical Takeaways.</span> These results illuminate both the potential and limitations of LLM-driven agents in navigating intra-session personalization. When user goals remain semantically adjacent—as in the case of Persona A—carryover of contextual knowledge can enable meaningful continuity. However, for users with evolving or multidimensional goals, agents must learn to filter and reinterpret past signals rather than reapply them wholesale. More broadly, this challenge exposes the need for dynamic memory management in generative agents: the ability to store, weigh, and selectively re-engage past preferences depending on goal alignment. Simply retaining all prior information risks diluting precision. Future work should explore mechanisms—such as salience scoring, goal re-grounding prompts, or hierarchical user modeling—that help agents decide when and how to transfer knowledge across adjacent tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03984v1/figures/newplot.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/223;" width="476" height="223" alt=" Illustration">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Dynamic evaluation using LLM-simulated users across three personalization metrics (relevance, diversity, novelty) averaged over multiple personas and sessions. Scores range from 1 (low) to 5 (high), computed via structured LLM outputs.</span><span id="acmlabel4" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay"> Illustration</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Step 3: Dynamic Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating personalized agents requires a principled approach that not only examines qualitative examples but also offers quantitative metrics for broader assessment. To this end, we introduce a dynamic evaluation pipeline grounded in simulation. Specifically, we deploy a simulated user—instantiated as an LLM-driven agent (GPT-4o-mini)—to assess system outputs across multiple metrics. These evaluations are conducted <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">across all 120 simulated users</span> (60 female and 60 male profiles), and the scores reported in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3.3. Challenge 3: Cross-Task Item Recommendation for Session B ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> represent average values aggregated over the full user set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While earlier sections illustrated the agent’s behavior through detailed case studies, this section aims to provide a comprehensive snapshot of personalization evaluation at scale. The evaluation is grounded in the agent’s performance across three distinct interaction contexts: (1) Session A (initial interaction), (2) Session B (follow-up session), and (3) Cross-task B (goal-shifted sub-task within the same session). These are operationalized through our structured simulation framework.Each output recommendation list is evaluated by the LLM-based simulated user using three personalization metrics:
(1.)<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relevance</span>: How well the items align with the user’s stated preferences. (2.) <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity</span>: Topical or functional variety among the top-<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> items. (3.) <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novelty</span>: The degree to which items are unexpected or go beyond prior known preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The simulated user is prompted to rate each metric on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = low, 5 = high), with justifications provided in natural language. To ensure consistency, we use deterministic prompting (temperature = 0) with fixed seeds where applicable. We also manually verified a subset of completions for coherence and fidelity. For each of the 120 user simulations, scores were computed across the three scenarios listed above, resulting in <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="120\times 3=360" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>120</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>360</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">120\times 3=360</annotation></semantics></math> evaluation trials. The final results presented in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3.3. Challenge 3: Cross-Task Item Recommendation for Session B ‣ 4.3. Step 2: Item Recommendation ‣ 4. Operationalizing Dynamic Evaluation: A Case Study in Shopping ‣ Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> are averaged over all trials, ensuring a stable and interpretable estimate of agent performance. Normalization was applied at the session level to mitigate variability due to differences in product space or user verbosity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our results reveal several key trends in agent performance across the three interaction contexts. In <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Session A</span> (Initial Task), relevance is highest with an average score of 4.63, indicating that the agent effectively incorporates elicited preferences into its initial recommendations. However, diversity (3.33) and novelty (2.97) are comparatively lower, suggesting that the agent tends to focus narrowly on explicitly stated user needs. In <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Session B</span>, relevance remains strong at 4.45, while diversity (3.42) and novelty (3.05) show slight improvements—reflecting more exploratory behavior as the agent gains confidence in inferred user preferences. Finally, in <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-task B</span>, relevance reaches its peak at 4.75, suggesting that the agent successfully transfers contextual knowledge from prior subtasks. Yet, novelty experiences a slight decline (2.95), indicating a stronger reliance on previously learned preferences rather than introducing new or unexpected options.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These trends reflect a desirable trade-off between exploitation and exploration. The agent is able to preserve alignment with user goals while incrementally introducing diversity and novelty over time. Nonetheless, the modest novelty scores highlight opportunities for future improvement, such as personalized diversification strategies or reinforcement-driven sampling techniques.
This section complements our qualitative demonstrations by providing large-scale evidence of the agent’s adaptive capabilities. The evaluation design—combining simulated feedback, structured metrics, and session-aware aggregation—enables a robust and reproducible measure of dynamic personalization effectiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper advances a perspective on the evaluation of personalized agents that moves beyond the confines of static benchmarks and one-off metrics. Rather than introducing a fixed framework or an empirical method, we offer a reorientation in how agent adaptability should be conceptualized and assessed. By anchoring evaluation in temporally grounded simulations, structured preference elicitation, and multi-session user modeling, our work articulates a generalizable paradigm for understanding personalization as a longitudinal, interaction-driven process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge that our current instantiation reflects a bounded scenario—focused on two sessions and two subtasks within a single domain. While this controlled setup provides clarity and reproducibility, future extensions may involve longer temporal trajectories, multi-agent coordination, or cross-domain personalization. Furthermore, our reliance on simulated LLM-based users and structured prompting opens avenues for richer user modeling, but also limits the realism of user variability and engagement. We do not, at this stage, explore mechanisms for memory summarization, adaptive forgetting, or scalable feedback aggregation. These are not shortcomings of the paradigm itself, but practical constraints of this initial demonstration. As these components mature, we anticipate that the evaluation paradigm outlined here will become more extensible, robust, and deployable across real-world systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking forward, this perspective paves the way for a broad set of future research trajectories. First, our work highlights the need to move beyond static personalization toward proactive agent behavior, where systems can anticipate, rather than merely react to, evolving user needs. This calls for novel mechanisms that detect early signals of intent drift, calibrate proactivity to user receptiveness, and balance initiative-taking with user autonomy. Second, the role of personalization-aware memory warrants deeper investigation. Agents must not only store prior interactions, but also develop dynamic strategies for retention, abstraction, and selective forgetting—ensuring that long-term personalization remains relevant without becoming redundant or brittle. This raises open questions around memory architectures, temporal salience scoring, and hybrid episodic-semantic storage for conversational agents. Third, as personalization grows more continuous and embedded across domains, there is a pressing need for multi-session, multi-task, and multi-domain generalization benchmarks that account for sustained adaptation. Our work lays a foundation for such evaluation, but substantial datasets and methodological advances are needed to scale simulation fidelity, enable interleaved tasks, and model life-long user trajectories. Collectively, these directions underscore that personalization is not merely a model optimization challenge—it is a systems-level, longitudinal, and deeply human-centered design problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.acknowledgements1" class="ltx_acknowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_acknowledgements">Acknowledgements.</h6>
This work was supported by the University of Washington eScience Institute in partnership with Microsoft Azure.

</div>
